---
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import cover from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/cover.png';
import coverCircle from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/cover-circle.png';
import statement from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/statement.png';
import references from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/references.png';
import extracurriculars from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/extracurriculars.png';
import recommend from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/recommend.png';
import signature from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/signature.png';
import notFound from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/not-found.png';
import phoneCircle from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/phone-circle.png';
import phoneTrailer from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/phone-trailer.png';
import phoneReferences from '../../assets/projects/dark-circles/phone-references.png';

const desktopSizes = '(min-width: 1000px) 960px, calc(100vw - 32px)';
const pairSizes = '(min-width: 1000px) 470px, (min-width: 900px) 45vw, calc(100vw - 32px)';
const phoneSizes = '(min-width: 760px) 230px, (min-width: 600px) 30vw, 64vw';

const parts = [
  { name: 'Cover page', text: 'The title, Roya’s applicant fields, and the three things to do: watch the trailer, donate, follow.' },
  { name: 'Section 1 · Personal statement', text: 'The premise, written as her admissions essay and corrected in marker, with the trailer clipped on as Attachment A.' },
  { name: 'Section 2 · References', text: 'The cast and crew as ID cards paper-clipped to the file, each flipping over to a bio.' },
  { name: 'Section 3 · Extracurriculars', text: 'Clips from the show’s TikTok and Instagram, its social accounts, and the fundraiser video from YouTube.' },
  { name: 'Section 4 · Letters of recommendation', text: 'The four donation tiers, from a name in the end credits to an Executive Producer credit.' },
  { name: 'Signature', text: 'The footer: the certification, Roya’s signature at 3:12 a.m., the social links, and the credits.' },
];

const phoneScreens = [
  { src: phoneCircle, alt: 'The cover page on a phone, with a finger held on the title revealing a dark circle and a word in Farsi underneath' },
  { src: phoneTrailer, alt: 'The trailer on a phone: a vertical video of Roya in a white attachment frame, with a round play button and sound toggle' },
  { src: phoneReferences, alt: 'The references section on a phone: two columns of cast ID cards, one flipped over to show the character’s bio' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  name="Dark Circles"
  description="Case study: the website Sām Kazemi designed and built in Astro for Dark Circles, a 30-episode vertical dark comedy created by Awni Abdi-Bahri with Violent Delights Productions."
  summary="The website for Dark Circles, a 30-episode vertical dark comedy: the lead character’s flawless medical-school application, with her unfiltered inner voice scrawled in the margins and a dark circle that cuts through the paper to show it."
  role="Website Developer"
  org="Violent Delights Productions"
  period="Oct 2026 – Present"
  status="Built; launch planned"
  stack={[
    'Astro',
    'TypeScript',
    'Modern CSS (@property, masks)',
    'Pointer Events',
    'HTML5 video',
    'ffmpeg',
    'Self-hosted fonts',
    'Responsive design',
    'Accessible UI',
    'Design systems',
  ]}
>
  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p>
    <h2>A pitch that has to sound like the show</h2>
    <p>
      Dark Circles follows Roya Delshad, an Iranian-American woman juggling medical school, her
      family’s expectations, and a secret acting career, while the audience hears every unfiltered
      thought in her head. It is a 30-episode vertical series created by Awni Abdi-Bahri with
      Violent Delights Productions, and it is independently funded.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the site has three jobs, in order: get people to watch the trailer, get them to donate, and
      get them to follow. It also has to read like the show. A crowdfunding template with a hero
      video and three feature cards would have done the first three jobs and failed the last one.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={cover}
        alt="The Dark Circles cover page: an application form with Roya’s name and grades, a paper-clipped headshot, the title with Circles ringed in red marker, and buttons to watch the trailer or donate"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>The cover page: Roya’s application, with her inner voice already in the margins.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The concept</p>
    <h2>Perfect on paper, spiraling in private</h2>
    <p>
      Sām mocked up three directions as working pages: Roya’s flawless medical-school application
      with Inner Roya scrawling over it, one sleepless night on her phone where scrolling moves the
      clock toward sunrise, and the key art come alive in neon bulbs and a letterboard. The
      application won, because its layout is the show’s tagline: perfect on paper, spiraling in
      private.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every section has two layers. The official one is the application: paper, a serif, and
      monospaced form labels, formal and dry. The private one is Inner Roya: red marker, rotated,
      lowercase or all caps, and sometimes Farsi. The jokes live in the gap between them, and red
      belongs to her alone, so anything red on the page is something she wrote.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before a page was built, the direction was written down as a design system: color tokens with
      checked contrast ratios, a type scale across four typefaces, a spacing scale, components,
      motion rules, the voice, and a list of hard bans. Everything on the site follows it, and
      anything it doesn’t cover gets added there first.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={statement}
        alt="The personal statement: a struck-through opening line corrected in red marker, the premise and Roya’s current circumstances, and the trailer in a white attachment frame"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The personal statement, corrected in marker, with the trailer clipped on as Attachment A.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The signature interaction</p>
    <h2>A dark circle through the paper</h2>
    <p>
      Under the cover page sits a second, ink-dark layer of Inner Roya’s notes. Moving the mouse cuts
      a soft-edged circle through the paper that follows the pointer, so the visitor reads what she
      is really thinking, a few words at a time. On a phone, pressing and holding opens the circle
      under the finger, and vertical swipes still scroll the page.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hole is a CSS mask: a radial gradient centered on the pointer, whose radius is a
      registered custom property so it can grow and shrink with a transition instead of snapping. On
      a first visit, the circle drifts across the title once to teach the gesture, and never again
      that session. The buttons sit above both layers, so the circle can’t hide them, and the ink
      layer is decoration hidden from screen readers: everything that matters is also on the paper.
      With reduced motion turned on, the peek and the marker animations are skipped, and the circle
      still follows the pointer.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={coverCircle}
        alt="The cover page with a dark circle cut through the paper over the title, showing a word in Farsi written in marker on the ink layer underneath"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>The same cover with the circle open over the title, and Inner Roya’s Farsi underneath.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The application</p>
    <h2>One page, filed in order</h2>
    <p>
      The site is a single page laid out like the application itself, with the primary actions in
      the order the show needs them.
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-flow">
      {
        parts.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <div>
              <strong>{p.name}</strong>
              <span>{p.text}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Video</p>
    <h2>Video without the platforms’ players</h2>
    <p>
      The trailer came in as a 121 MB file. Sām re-encoded it with ffmpeg into an 8.1 MB version
      for desktops and a 4.1 MB one for phones, chosen by screen width, and both start playing
      before they finish downloading. Its poster is a real frame from the trailer, and the player is
      the site’s own: the play button is literally a dark circle, and play, pause, and sound are
      labeled buttons. “Watch the trailer” on the cover glides down to it, stops if the visitor
      scrolls, and lands focus on the play button.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of the platforms’ embed scripts load. Clips are the show’s own files, listed in one data
      file and shown as vertical tiles that preview silently on hover and toggle on tap, each linking
      back to its original post. The YouTube fundraiser video is a thumbnail until it’s clicked, and
      only then loads YouTube’s privacy-enhanced player.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={extracurriculars}
        alt="The extracurriculars section: a vertical teaser clip with a caption, the show’s Instagram, TikTok, and YouTube accounts, and the fundraiser video as a click-to-load thumbnail"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>Extracurriculars: the teaser clip, the show’s accounts, and the fundraiser video.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Cast and crew</p>
    <h2>References that flip</h2>
    <p>
      The cast and crew appear as their ID cards, paper-clipped to the file at slightly different
      angles. Hovering for half a second, clicking, or tapping flips a card to the bio on its back.
      Each card is a real button that reports whether it’s flipped, with the bio linked as its
      description, so keyboard and screen-reader users get the same story. The back of the card
      resizes its own type with container queries, so a bio still fits a card half a phone wide.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={references}
        alt="The references section: cast ID cards paper-clipped in a grid, with the middle card flipped to show the character’s bio"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>The references, one card flipped to its bio.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Donations</p>
    <h2>Every donation is a letter of recommendation</h2>
    <p>
      The four donation tiers are laid out as checkboxes on the form, from Roya’s Inner Circle at
      $100 to The Dark Circle at $5,000 and up. Payment happens on the fiscal sponsor’s donation
      page, so the site never handles a card, and the note under the button names the sponsor and
      its tax ID, so donors know the gift is tax-deductible.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={recommend}
        alt="The letters of recommendation section: a sign-and-donate button and four donation tiers with checkboxes, prices, and perks"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>Four tiers, from a name in the end credits to an Executive Producer credit.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Voice</p>
    <h2>The voice reaches every surface</h2>
    <p>
      The official layer is written like an admissions form, and Inner Roya is short, specific, and
      unfiltered. The design system holds both to that, down to buttons, alt text, and a list of
      banned marketing phrases. The footer is the application’s signature block, and the 404 page
      is a status update: this page didn’t get in either.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot cs-shot--wide">
      <div class="cs-pair">
        <Screenshot
          src={signature}
          alt="The footer as a signature block: a certification line with (mostly) in marker, Roya’s signature, the date 3:12 a.m., and the social links"
          widths={[600, 900, 1400]}
          sizes={pairSizes}
          class="cs-screen"
        />
        <Screenshot
          src={notFound}
          alt="The 404 page: This page didn’t get in either, with get in struck through in red and a marker note, it applied though, it applied so hard"
          widths={[600, 900, 1400]}
          sizes={pairSizes}
          class="cs-screen"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption>The signature block at the end of the page, and the 404.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">On a phone</p>
    <h2>Designed at 375 pixels first</h2>
    <p>
      A vertical series is watched on phones, so the site was designed at phone width first. The
      title is sized by both the screen’s width and its height, so the whole cover, buttons
      included, fits above the fold. The hint under the buttons changes from “rub the page” to
      “press &amp; hold the page” on touch screens, and the vertical trailer fills the width of the
      screen.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <div class="cs-phones">
        {phoneScreens.map((s) => <Screenshot src={s.src} alt={s.alt} widths={[300, 500, 700]} sizes={phoneSizes} class="cs-screen cs-phone" />)}
      </div>
      <figcaption>The circle under a finger, the trailer, and a flipped reference card on a phone.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Engineering highlights</p>
    <h2>Light, private, and native</h2>
    <div class="cs-features">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="layers" /></span>
        <h3>Design system first</h3>
        <p>
          Colors, type, spacing, components, motion, and voice are written down before they’re built,
          and the stylesheet’s tokens mirror that document, so the site and its rules can’t drift
          apart.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="target" /></span>
        <h3>A performance budget</h3>
        <p>
          The page has a 1.5 MB budget before any video plays. Fonts are self-hosted subsets with
          the title’s font preloaded, the cast cards and trailer poster are resized WebP, and clip
          videos don’t load until they play.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="lock" /></span>
        <h3>No platform embeds</h3>
        <p>
          No TikTok, Instagram, or YouTube script loads with the page, so reading it sends nothing
          to those platforms. YouTube’s privacy-enhanced player loads only when someone presses
          play.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="code" /></span>
        <h3>Motion without libraries</h3>
        <p>
          The circle, the marker that draws itself around the title, and the card flips use CSS
          transitions and animations, a registered custom property, and requestAnimationFrame. The only runtime
          dependencies are Astro and the fonts.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="users" /></span>
        <h3>Accessible by default</h3>
        <p>
          Real buttons with labels, a visible red focus ring, decorative layers hidden from screen
          readers, reduced-motion support, and no horizontal scrolling at 375 pixels.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="file-text" /></span>
        <h3>Content as data</h3>
        <p>
          Clips come from one data file with each post’s platform, link, video, poster, and caption,
          and the cast is one list, so adding a clip or a cast member is one entry.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">What’s next</p>
    <h2>What launch still needs</h2>
    <p>
      The site launches once the domain and hosting are settled. After that, more of the show’s clips join the extracurriculars, and a fifth
      section, the transcript, lists the episodes once the series is released.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
